swarren@convex.com (Steve Warren) writes:
>steve@wintermute.ucsd.edu ({Darkavich}) writes:
>>cerebus@ucrmath.UUCP(edward tyrrill)writes:
>>solovay-andrew@CS.YALE.EDU (Andrew Solovay) writes:
>>	It might be nice, in future, to have an 'appraise' command.  The usage
>>	would be:  While in a shop (perhaps only while next to the
>>	shopkeeper), you use the extended #appraise command, and specify an
>>	item; the shopkeeper then tells you what he would pay for that item.

>>I believe he was asking about an appraise command for items in you
>>inventory, so that you do not have to drop them to find out what they are
>>worth.
 
>OK, then why not loose the "pay" command, and instead replace it with
>"settle-up".  When you drop things the shopkeeper says:
  
>           "I'll give 37 for that worthless rusted longsword."
 
>When you pick things up he tells you the charge for them as usual.  When
>you settle-up everything is paid both ways at the same time.  This would
>also allow you to drop items several times, trying to get the best price
>for them.

I've got a better idea (slightly), in my opinion...if you guys didn't 
like my #chat extension idea I posted a while back, then how about
when you drop items, the shop-keeper tells you how much he'd pay you
for it without buying as it says above.  Instead of haveing one
'settle-up' command, have 'pay' and 'sell' commands.  This will allow
some easier strategy to develop when decided what to buy/sell and what 
not to buy/sell than it would with 'resolve everything'.  You'd only
have to think about what's on the floor as far as selling goes and
what's in your pack as far as paying goes instead of having to figure
out how much is in both places, etc.  The only problem with this is
that it requires YAK (yet another key:-) unless it's an extended
command...

